=== GENERAL FEEDBACK ===

Kindly take note of these general principles moving forward - these are essential to your growth as a data scientist/programmer. Most of my feedback moving forward are based on these.

- [DOCUMENTATION] There was sparse documentation throughout the scripts, which made them difficult to review and understand, and which also makes you liable for remembering every line and detail (and we don't want that). Docstrings are necessary for a general understanding of the function but must be accompanied by more specific code comments throughout to make the code itself comprehensible. These must be placed as the code is written (not after-the-fact!), just in case you forget or the code needs to be reviewed by someone else.

- [PARAMETRIZATION] There are multiple functions doing very similar things for different objects. This (a) makes the code bloated with repetition, and (b) opens up more room for error since modifications applied to one function may not be carried out to all the others that are doing something of the same. Lump these into unique functions, with parameters that can vary based on the use case.

- [COMPARTMENTALIZATION] Avoid any functions that overwrite the entire data table. At most, these functions must output columns/vectors that are then placed in the dt. Overwriting the dt across multiple steps (a) opens up more room for error since the entire dt is open to modification within the function (no matter how carefully you write the function to deal with only certain columns.. we are still people capable of error), and (b) consumes significant amounts of memory and runtime.

- [COMPREHENSIVENESS] This more for the documentation - steps like the deduplication of ICD-10 codes should have shown up on there also. Rather than writing up each step in the code then creating the write-up from there, I suggest you take a step back and list own the *broader* steps you've had to go through before proceeding. Kindly refer back to my previous write-ups as a writing sample.


